  (text "Power key (2026-11-18/19, user's request).\n\nT16 - top row, last column, the orange power keycap - is the Commander's power button.  The keyboard microcontroller\nreads it and pulls the mainboard's RK817 power-key line (PMIC_PWRON) low through Q201, over a 2-wire cable from J202\nto the mainboard's J22, the way a PC's front-panel button reaches its motherboard; the RK817's own logic does the\nrest (press: on; short press while on: power-key event; about 6 s held: off).  USB stays a standard USB-C (J201), so\nthat after the device's life the keyboard can be reused on its own as a USB keyboard (then J202 just stays empty and\nthe firmware treats T16 as an ordinary key).  The keyboard is powered while the board is off: its USB 5 V comes from\nthe mainboard's VCC5V_BUS (behind the PoE / battery selector), not from a rail the PMIC switches.\n\nStandby: the 84 infra-red LEDs are all switched by Q1 (about 0.56 A); for the power key alone that is far too much.\nT16's LED is therefore taken off the shared LED_K and hung on its own row line A0: it lights exactly while row 1 is\ndriven low - during the normal scan of row 1, which is when T16 is read anyway (wait ~30 us after driving the row) -\nand in standby the firmware wakes every ~20 ms, drives A0 low for ~100 us with Q1 off, reads T16 on MOSI and sleeps:\nabout 6.8 mA x 100 us / 20 ms = ~35 uA.  (A0 then sinks T16's LED, ~6.8 mA, plus the row's photo-transistors.)" (exclude_from_sim no) (at 15.24 25.4 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.524 1.524)) (justify left top)) (uuid "7436ba4b-9092-58df-a53a-898c8aa2523b"))
